<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abdi-Ashirta</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">14th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century-bce">14th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abdi-Ashirta was the ruler of Amurru, a land in the mountains and coast of present-day northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a> and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, in the fourteenth century BCE. Nominally a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, he seized neighbouring towns and threatened <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byblos">Byblos</a>, whose king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rib-hadda">Rib-Hadda</a> denounced him repeatedly in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amarna-letters">Amarna letters</a>. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aziru">Aziru</a> continued his expansion and later brought Amurru over to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittites</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abdi-ashirta.jpg" alt="Abdi-Ashirta" width="320" height="342" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Towns_of_aram.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…the fourteenth century BCE: King Rib-Hadda wrote more than sixty letters to the pharaohs Amenhotep III and Akhenaten, pleading for archers and supplies as <b>Abdi-Ashirta</b> of Amurru and then his son Aziru seized neighbouring towns, cut off the city’s grain supply and turned its own people against him.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byblos">Byblos · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 14th century BCE <b>Abdi-Ashirta</b> united them into a kingdom at the expense of Egyptian vassals, and his son Aziru captured Simyra and made it his capital, despite angry letters from Rib-Hadda of Byblos.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-amurru">Kingdom of Amurru</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Abdi-Ashirta</b> and his son Aziru seized it, and it became the capital of the Kingdom of Amurru.</blockquote><a href="/state/simyra">Simyra</a></li></ul></section>
